_yest":"The high of <strong>53\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 59\u00b0F, and the record is 76\u00b0F (1938, 1955). The low of <strong>43\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Record highs and lows, by the decade that set them","p_rec":"If Salisbury's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Salisbury has set <strong>87 standing record lows</strong>, close to the 82 that chance alone would give, and <strong>89 record highs</strong>, close to the 82 that chance alone would give. The 1910s hold the most record highs of any decade (48). Salisbury's all-time high is 106\u00b0F, on August 7, 1918.","t_chg":"Salisbury's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 111 complete years: Salisbury's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.10\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.02 to +0.18), about +1.2\u00b0F since 1908. Salisbury's average high shows no clear change: +0.02\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.07 to +0.10) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.75\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.30 to +1.21) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.53\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.18 to +0.89). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 3 of 12 months. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months; May fell.","t_rain":"Salisbury has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Salisbury's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.54 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.00 to +1.08), about +6.1 inches since 1913. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 47.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 1948 (72.86 in); the driest, 1930 (21.67 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 10 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}